candidates.generation_role:
Deliberately light, so the results can be trusted. On the fly, Claude:
- maps a term that wasn't precomputed to the nearest precomputed terms and period synonyms ('reliability diagram' → 'calibration curve');
- classifies false friends from subjects and snippets;
- optionally writes a 2-3 sentence gloss of a selected spike, or of 'what these pages say'. It is built only from the visible text, its line pointers are verified, and it is labelled AI.

Everything else is retrieval. Fallback: local fuzzy matching to the nearest precomputed term, and no glosses. The static wiki build can query Search Inside live for any term; the artifact uses only the precomputed set.

Precomputed with Claude at build: the full claims ledger and the showcase proposition ledgers.

Live:
- extraction from newly pasted or selected papers;
- relevance, stance, span, evidence type, LR and dependence for new propositions;
- value-of-information study proposals;
- Q&A through query_ledger, where Claude may only cite claims the tool returns.

Deterministic: retrieval, Bayes arithmetic in log-odds, the dependence discount, span and number verification, and all charts.

Fallback: the precomputed ledgers with every chart, filter and export working, plus a manual mode where the reader adds papers through BM25 search and sets every LR.

On the fly, Claude:
- resolves messy or ambiguous citations among local candidates ('Gelman BDA3' becomes Bayesian Data Analysis, 3rd ed., QA279.5 .G45 2014);
- writes one-line routing reasons;
- suggests free shadows, restricted to the local corpora and the curated list;
- explains in plain language a key's trade-offs and the cheapest-path choice.

Deterministic: routing, totals, door logic, the advisor and the optimizer.

Precomputed: routes for every catalog item, and the skill tags.

Fallback: regex and heuristic parsing plus fuzzy matching, templated reasons and precomputed shadows. The desk stays fully functional, and unresolved items stay in the tray.

On the fly, Claude does:
- query planning and tool calls;
- passage selection and ordering, turn roles, and alignment by argument move;
- 'responds to' links;
- editor's notes and gutter glosses (25 words at most, in a different typeface, labelled AI);
- the counter-voice pass.

Deterministic: all quotation text, verification, reuse arcs, page mapping and layout.

Scores are cached by (question, corpus version, model), and popular ones ship as JSON.

Fallback on the static wiki: the precomputed conversations in all three layouts, plus a no-LLM concordance mode that lists keyword-in-context lines for the typed terms in publication order.

On the fly, Claude writes:
- scouting reports, naming only works in the hex payload;
- an optional expedition, a 5-hex route through the frontier;
- extra retrieval 'probes', proposing vocabulary drawn from your keepers;
- one-line explanations of regions where the fog glows;
- 'make the case' notes for and against a card;
- graded hints in Guess mode.

Precomputed: layout, aggregates, frontier scores and fragments.

Fallback: templated reports (capital = most-edited work; what is free here; oldest; a first line), the three mechanical probes only, and templated hints. The map, foraging maths, estimator and scoring are client code and work fully without Claude. Generated text is labelled.

On the table, generation is heavy. Claude runs the reference interview, selects books through tools under the constraints, and streams talkers and pitch cards as JSON ({id, role, talker, quote_ref}). The page drops any unknown id and any quote that doesn't match the text.

In the Delve, Claude writes short 'why here' lines and encounter narration from the two linked records only, plus the end-of-run story. Map generation, draws, relics and scoring are deterministic.

Fallback:
- the ~20 precomputed tables, chosen by nearest theme;
- a deterministic 'table from a shelf': neighbours of a chosen book, plus one free classic and one arXiv paper, with templated cards;
- templated Delve connections, e.g. 'Shares "Bayesian statistical decision theory"; 126 years earlier'.

Live and central. Through sample, Claude uses page tools (search_passages(work, query), get_lines(work, start, end), neighbors) to propose:
- the glossary of shared terms and the sub-questions;
- stances and quote line ranges;
- fact/definition/value labels and oppositions;
- the formal model and the 'what would change their mind' column.
The page, not Claude, extracts and string-verifies every quote. Output streams, so cells fill progressively.

Fallback: the precomputed tables, in the identical UI. For a new question offline, the TileBars and the top passages by BM25, plus a manual mode where the reader drags passages into the grid.

On the fly, Claude:
- chooses lenses and generates specs (nodes with line pointers and anchors);
- writes formulas for numerical claims;
- edits specs from plain-language requests;
- writes self-test questions.

Deterministic: schema validation, pointer verification, arithmetic, rendering and two-way linking.

Fallback: precomputed lenses for the showcase chapters. Other chapters get only deterministic lenses (a list of numbers found by regex, a cast list of capitalised names, an outline from headings), with a note that generated lenses need the artifact runtime.

On the fly, Claude uses page tools (search, shelf_neighbors(call, k), get_work, getHits(term)) to:
- write misshelf slips and 'range guide' notes for bays;
- turn a fuzzy question into a lamp of subjects and terms ('Brier' → 'quadratic scoring rule');
- find a book's strongest critic from a 40-candidate shortlist, showing the top 3 rather than one;
- propose aisle taxonomies, make batched aisle assignments with confidence, and write shelf-talkers.

Every cited id is checked against the embedded data.

Precomputed: geometry, glow index, scatter statistics, fore-edge hits and hero threads.

Fallback: a lexical lamp; precomputed slips, threads and reshelvings; and the fixed orders (Dewey, year, popularity, editions, access).

On the fly, Claude writes:
- briefs restricted to the chosen period, through year-capped tools;
- redline narration and period-vocabulary rewrites;
- word senses for the river (sense clusters with glosses, and an assignment of each line to a sense, as JSON);
- 'before the word' phrasings;
- retro-forecast questions;
- amber sense-drift proposals, each of which must show period KWIC lines as evidence.
A second pass looks for claims the cited period works don't support.

Deterministic: date filtering, shelf growth, river counts, red-flag linting, quote verification and Brier scoring.

Fallback:
- precomputed briefs at fixed epochs, with the slider snapping to them;
- precomputed showcase rivers, and an uncoloured count river for any other term;
- the linter still runs live on any pasted text.

On the fly, Claude:
- extracts predictions from any embedded public-domain chapter, streaming JSON cards. The page verifies each quote by exact match and drops any it can't verify.
- operationalises each prediction;
- maps hedge words to probabilities, showing the mapping so it can be overridden;
- proposes resolutions with a confidence and an evidence summary, labelled 'AI-proposed, unreviewed' until a human accepts them.

Fallback: the 5 precomputed audited books, plus the Hindsight Test on them. Without sample, other books show only a hedge-word density scan computed in the page.
